  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/0/0a/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_23.png",
  "plot_summary": "When Reed snaps at the group for not paying attention to Dr. Doom's time machine (which they have recently transferred to the Baxter Building lab for study), thus allowing a baby dinosaur to suddenly appear out of it, they all decide it's time to elect a new leader!\n\nThe next couple of pages show someone at a courthouse, bailing criminals out of jail.&nbsp; Three in particular are specially hand-picked by what turns out to be a robot.&nbsp; [HANG] * Bull Brogan for his brawn\n[HANG] * \"Handsome\" Harry Phillips for his cunning con man-like brain\n[HANG] * Yogi Dakor, a \"fire-proof\" man from a circus\n\nThe three have been chosen for a special mission:&nbsp; to assist... <b>Dr. Doom</b> in destroying the Fantastic Four!&nbsp; Only by eliminating <i>them</i> will the world \"bow to Doctor Doom!\"\n\nBack in the FF's lab, the other three members of the FF try to decide who should be the new leader, only to find that each one has voted <i>themselves</i> leader.&nbsp; It seems there will be no consensus by ballot, and of <i>course</i> Ben and Johnny start fighting over it (consuming most of three pages) before Sue steps in with an invisible force field and stops their antics.&nbsp; Finally, Reed shows up and shakes his head.&nbsp; It seems only <i>he</i> has the maturity to lead this cast of characters, and he orders them to clean up the mess while he focuses on locating Doom, whom he knows is still out there!\n\nBut Doom is busy giving extra powers to each of the three he freed from jail, one of which is flame-proofing, and he tempts Johnny to come forward by offering to give a one million dollar car to the Human Torch!&nbsp; Like an idiot, he completely falls for it and is captured!\n\nMeanwhile, Ben is angered by a nasty letter from the Yancy Street Gang, and he storms over to clobber them, but one of the other three of Doom's gang is waiting, and his strength has been multiplied many times over now.&nbsp; As they tangle, he shoots Ben with a cosmic ray beam that transforms him back to Ben Grimm, and he too is captured!\n\nAt the same time, Reed pretends to attack Sue to test her ability to defend herself, but she's still mad at him and heads home where flunkie #3 is waiting to give her flowers laced with a sleeping gas, but Sue senses the treachery and turns invisible, but the man, now enhanced with super hearing, can tell where she is.&nbsp; He sprays her with ether mist, and she finally succumbs.\n\nAll that's left is Mr. Fantastic, who Doom will tackle personally!&nbsp; Reed responds to an FF flare, thinking he sees Ben calling to him below, but it's actually a Thing robot, and soon he's trapped in a plexiglass case, the prisoner of Dr. Doom!&nbsp; Doom has his prisoners now, and he disposes of the three flunkies by transporting them to another dimension \"until your master has need for you again!\"\n\nBut Doom does not know about Sue's new power to cast invisible force fields, and she uses it to help Ben escape, who then smashes everyone else out.&nbsp; They battle Doom, but to no avail, as Doom tries to maneuver them toward a \"solar wave\" which moves toward them and threatens to send them out into space!&nbsp; But Sue extends her force shield around Doom, ensuring that if they die, so will he!&nbsp; He has no choice but to try and disable the device!&nbsp; Ben then grabs him then and hurls him into space, and with a cry of \"HELLLLLP...!\"&nbsp; Doom is sent flying away into space!&nbsp; They barely have time themselves to crawl through the hole Doom came into the room before it, too, is completely claimed by space.\n\nBen is so grateful Reed knew what a solar wave was that he says Reed can remain the leader!",
  "grade_one_liner": "The villain is repetitive, the flunkies disposable, and the solar waves are nonsense.\nSomehow, the characters still manage to save the issue.",
  "subtitle": "Now Hiring:&nbsp; A New Villain",
  "review_text": "[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=System Error:]\n[HANG] You've reached your annual Doctor Doom quota.\n[HANG] Please try one of the following alternatives:\n[HANG2] * Mad Thinker\n[HANG2] * Puppet Master\n[HANG2] * Mole Man\n[HANG2] * Sub-Mariner\n\n<b>Marvel's response?</b>  [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=\"No.\"]\n\nIf <i>Fantastic Four</i> had modern software licensing rules, Doctor Doom would have been [SPAN: severity=error; text=locked out] of this book several issues ago.&nbsp; Instead, Marvel looked at its villain roster, shrugged, and concluded:\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=<i>Well</i>... one more couldn't hurt!]\n\nIt's as if Marvel owns twenty action figures, [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=but keeps playing with the same one!]\n\nEven <i>contemporary</i> readers were starting to notice.&nbsp; James Smith said it best in the letters page of next month's <i>Amazing Spider-Man </i>10 (ASM #10, while referring to [LINK:1963:ASM_5|ASM #5|2026-12-01], which <i>also</i> featured Doom):&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=\"...has Dr. Doom got a financial interest in your comics?&nbsp; He turns up with monotonous regularity every month.\"]\n\nEven Marvel acknowledges the frequent appearances in their <b>Special Announcements Section</b> at the end of [SPAN: severity=error; text=this very issue:]\n\n[INDENT: both | 3rem] <i>It's a funny thing -- we get countless letters each week asking for more and more of the Doc.... But then, when we DO repeat some of our characters, we get almost as many letters from other readers saying they're sick of seeing them and telling us to think up NEW foes each issue.&nbsp; Sometimes we think the best way to edit a mag might be by just tossing a coin!</i>\n\nObviously that \"coin\" must have landed on [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Doom...<i>again</i>\"] this time around... [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=\"<i>funny thing</i>...\"]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nDoom must have been very popular with contemporary readers, as this is his <i>sixth</i> appearance in the pages of <i>Fantastic Four</i> alone (out of the 23 total issues and one annual published thus far).\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=That's a full <i>quarter</i> of all books the title has published, which <i>does</i> feel like a lot.]\n\nFurthermore, even though it had been a full six months since his last appearance in <i>this </i>magazine, he had appeared as recently as three months earlier, in the pages of [LINK:1963:ASM_5|ASM #5|2026-12-01] (requiring Stan to explain how he survived the end of his [LINK:1963:FF_17|FF #17|2026-10-05] plummet toward the ground).&nbsp; For readers who buy <i>all</i> Marvel superhero mags (and not <i>just</i> FF), even <i>that</i> was reason enough for James Smith (see letter snippet above) to write in and speculate on whether Doom was an investor in Marvel Comics.\n\nFor me personally, my own journey through this time period means that I read these Silver Age books a bit more rapidly than people did back in the 1960s, so seeing the cover of this book, promising his latest \"Master Plan\" felt... <i>well</i>... perhaps a little <i>too</i> soon.&nbsp; Judging by my review dates on these issues, it has been only a two-month, real-world gap, so there is a little bit of [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Hey, didn't we just <i>see</i> this guy?\"] going on in my own mind.\n\nBe that as it may... Doom is here.&nbsp; And it needs to be treated like any other story...\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe book doesn't <i>start</i> with Doom though.&nbsp; Instead, it focuses on the members of the Fantastic Four bickering amongst themselves yet again, this time annoyed with the way Reed runs the group, even to the point of expressing irritation with them for not following his orders properly.\n[ASSET: center | TimeForANewLeader | width=67%]\n\nIt was enough to drive them to try and elect a new leader.&nbsp; Though this seemed like a [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=major overreaction,] the mayhem that followed did what Stan and Jack needed every month, which is to consume pages, not to mention feature what happens in just about every issue:&nbsp; The Human Torch battles the Thing (for the umpteenth time -- see my reaction to this same battle in last month's [LINK:1963:ST_116_torch1|ST #116torch|2027-02-20]).\n\nThis only added to the [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=\"Not <i>again</i>!\"] reaction, and in the end, prevented the book from scoring higher.\n\nBut none of that was even part of Doom's \"Master Plan!\"\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | YouWillServeDoom | width=22%] Doom decides he needs flunkies to do his dirty work for him (perhaps to conceal his involvement) and bails out three lowlifes from prison.\n[BR]\n[BR] I have to say that the Doom we see here (and the one we saw in [LINK:1963:FF_16|FF #16|2026-09-14], [LINK:1963:FF_17|FF #17|2026-10-05] and [LINK:1963:ASM_5|ASM #5|2026-12-01]) strikes me as more \"petty\" (and less mysterious) than how he was originally portrayed when first introduced, [SPAN: severity=error; text=far more concerned with destroying the FF than anything else] (though his plan to make the world \"bow to Doctor Doom\" seems right out of the super-villain playbook, so you can't say he isn't consistent in that regard).\n\nIn some aspects, he seems like nothing more than a very powerful, very formidable super-villain, so we'll have to see how he is developed going forward, but these last appearances have done nothing to elevate his character outside of what we already knew:&nbsp; he's obviously brilliant, clever, and seems to have unlimited resources (recall his puzzling lack of concern over the destruction of his castle back in [LINK:1962:FF_5|FF #5|2026-03-29], which I pointed out).\n\nWhile this diminished my ultimate enjoyment of the story, I still found it a solid effort overall and worthy of the grade it received.&nbsp; Furthermore, Doom's treatment of his three flunkies seemed [SPAN: severity=success; text=completely in character.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThough pages 10-15 were little more than two pages each of the Human Torch, the Thing, and eventually the Invisible Girl falling prey to Doom's three flunkies, leaving Mr. Fantastic to him personally, they also highlighted one of the book's greatest strengths:&nbsp; the personalities of the cast.\n\n[ASSET: left | GoPolishATestTube | width=13%] [ASSET: right | WorldWillBeMine | width=20%] First, the page 14 exchange between Reed and Sue reminded me very much of what Jan might say to Hank.&nbsp; I laughed out loud when Sue told Reed, [SPAN: severity=success; text=\"Oh, go polish a test tube or something!\"]\n[BR]\n[BR] And the maniacal look of triumph on the face of Doom at the bottom of page 15 was [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=deliciously evil!]&nbsp; Jack did a [SPAN: severity=success; text=<i>particularly</i> good job] showing Doom's look of glee, even through the mask.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=\"The world will be mine!!\"]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Mwahahaha!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Saying that Reed is \"my equal,\" seems a surprising admission, something I also questioned back in issue 17.)]\n\n[ASSET: left | Cornball | width=25%] But the panel that showed even <i>Stan</i> seemed to understand the heightened melodrama of it all came on page 17, where the Thing quipped that Doom's speech made him seem like nothing more than a B-movie villain, who proclaimed victory over his adversaries just before [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"the hero <i>clobbers</i> him in the next scene!\"]\n[BR]\n[BR] It was the <i>perfect</i> way for Stan to wink at the audience and acknowledge that, \"Yeah, we <i>know</i> this is over the top!\"&nbsp; And using the Thing's personality to call that out was the [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=chef's kiss!]\n\nAnd what does Doom do next?&nbsp; Naturally, he eliminates his three \"helpers\" by sending them off to another dimension, there to remain \"until your master has need for you again!\"\n[ASSET: center | SentToAnotherDimension | width=67%]\n\nSay what you want about the story in general -- outside of the whole \"Reed Richards is a terrible leader,\" Stan's dialogue elevates the characterization to an entirely new level.\n\nThat's ultimately what saves the issue from being dragged down by its weaker plot elements.&nbsp; The personalities feel so distinct by this point that simply watching these characters interact remains entertaining even when the story itself starts leaning on questionable ideas.\n\nWas anyone surprised <i>at all</i> when Doom effectively waved his hands and sent his three flunkies packing to another dimension?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold-italic; text=I sure wasn't!]&nbsp; Unlike Spider-Man back in [LINK:1963:ASM_5|ASM #5|2026-12-01] (who he also tried to manipulate to help battle the FF), these characters didn't even earn enough respect to try and fool -- he just dismissed them!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=It was <i>classic Doom</i>.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nIt was nice to see Sue's invisible force field powers playing a major role in how the story eventually wound down, and as Johnny rightly pointed out on page 18, they're still new enough to be unknown by their opponents.\n\n[ASSET: left | DoomInSpaceAgain | width=13%] The inevitable final conflict with Doom resulted in a repeat trip into space (see [LINK:1962:FF_6|FF #6|2026-04-01] for his first).\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=But not to worry!]&nbsp; As the final panel indicates, \"Stan and Jack are already trying to figure out a way to bring him back!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Let's just hope <i>this</i> time takes more than six months!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBy <i>far</i> the weakest part of the story was the existence of these \"solar waves,\" which Doom claims on page 21 \"sweeps the Earth every twenty-four hours!\"&nbsp; Reed then goes on a long-winded explanation of what solar waves are, and the imagery that Jack includes [SPAN: severity=warning; text=suggests that the waves simply transport matter into space!]\n[ASSET: center | SolarWavesExplained | width=67%]\n\nThis seems a [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=terrifying phenomenon], and were it to be true, matter on Earth would simply <i>vanish</i> -- and this <b>repeated every 24 hours no less</b>!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Are you <i>kidding</i> me?)]\n\nObviously this cannot be literally true, or it would be making front-page news across the globe on an almost daily basis, which would make the Thing's claim about not knowing what they were on page 22 totally unbelievable.\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=Stan and Jack have accidentally introduced a world-shattering phenomenon that apparently nobody in the Marvel Universe has ever noticed... until Reed shows up to explain it!]\n\nI understand that readers are expected to suspend disbelief when reading these fantastic tales about superheroes and the Marvel Universe in general, [SPAN: severity=info; text=this is asking too much!]&nbsp; It is, without a doubt, a bridge too far, and as such, again the overall grade is capped.\n\nFortunately, the issue has enough personality elsewhere that this particular misfire doesn't sink the entire book.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nWhether you were a Dr. Doom fan or not didn't seem to matter this time around.&nbsp; Nothing was going to stop his latest \"Master Plan\" from happening... and the cover warned us all of this.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=Stan absolutely knocked the dialogue out of the park, and seeing Doom dismiss his three flunkies should not have surprised anyone.]\n\nYet... seeing Doom stoop to picking these three losers in the first place was head-scratching, [SPAN: severity=error; text=and the whole of the \"solar waves\" claim was patently ridiculous.]&nbsp; (And I could do with a little less roughhousing every issue out of Ben and Johnny.)\n\nFortunately, the book's strengths lie elsewhere.&nbsp; By this point, Stan and Jack have built such distinct personalities for the members of the Fantastic Four that [SPAN: severity=info; text=watching them interact remains enjoyable even when the plot occasionally goes off the rails.]\n\nThe developing personalities more than made up for the sour taste of the ending scenario, and I especially like the way the Thing is being characterized.\n\nThe net effect was... <i>well...</i> [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=\"fine.\"]\n[BR] So it should not surprise anyone; that's what it got.",
  "letters_page": "The letters page hints that Captain America might soon be joining the Avengers!\n[BR] There's also a letter solely about the X-Men, which seemed out of place.",
  "decoder_ring": "<b>Doom's Odd Respect for Reed Richards</b>\n\nFor someone obsessed with proving his superiority, Doom repeatedly describes Reed as his intellectual equal.&nbsp; Whether intended or not, it creates the impression that Reed is one of the few people in the world whose opinion Doom actually values... even while trying to destroy him.",
  "highlight": "Even a surplus of Doom can't overshadow the growing chemistry of the cast, though Reed has to pause to explain an Earth-shattering phenomenon nobody has apparently noticed before."
}